1.text:
The ionization chamber current of 2 pA is recorded directly at the focus (i.e., the same location occupied by tissue during imaging). This already incorporates all upstream effects of the bent-crystal monochromator, so the supplied 50 mm × 6 mm monochromator aperture dimensions are superfluous and must not be used to rescale fluence.  

Convert the measured current to absorbed power with the standard air W-value (33.97 J C⁻¹) to obtain ≈6.79 × 10⁻¹¹ W. The focused beam cross-section (0.3 mm × 6 mm) together with chamber length and air density fixes the irradiated air mass at 3.51 × 10⁻⁷ kg, yielding an air dose rate of ≈1.93 × 10⁻⁴ Gy s⁻¹. Because the problem states that μ_en/ρ(tissue) ≈ μ_en/ρ(air), the tissue dose rate is identical. Multiplication by the given 0.02 s scan factor then produces the cumulative surface dose of 3.87 µGy. (Photon energy and the explicit μ_en/ρ value are likewise unnecessary once the focused-beam ionization measurement is available.)

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3.87 µGy}

