3.text:
The chamber measurement occurs precisely at the subject plane, so the 2 pA current already embodies the post-monochromator fluence, compression, and any diffraction losses that will actually reach tissue. Consequently the supplied monochromator aperture, photon energy, and tabulated \(\mu_{en}/\rho\) are superfluous and cannot be re-introduced without double-counting.

Standard conversion therefore proceeds directly from the measured current: absorbed power \(P = 2 \times 10^{-12} \times 33.97 \approx 6.79 \times 10^{-11}\) W. Irradiated air mass inside the chamber is fixed by the focused-beam footprint, chamber length and density: \(m = 0.03 \times 0.6 \times 15.1 \times 1.293 \times 10^{-3} \approx 3.51 \times 10^{-7}\) kg. Division supplies an air dose rate of \(\approx 1.936 \times 10^{-4}\) Gy s\(^{-1}\). Because the problem states \(\mu_{en}/\rho\) (tissue) \(\approx \mu_{en}/\rho\) (air), the tissue rate is identical. Multiplication by the given 0.02 s scan factor then yields the cumulative surface dose 3.87 µGy.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3.87 µGy}

